Yes, that is exactly correct. Firmware is a specific type of embedded software. [1] 
While all firmware is embedded software, not all embedded software is firmware. [2, 3, 4] 
## The Key Differences

* Firmware: This is the most basic software that is permanently burned into a hardware chip (like a ROM or flash memory chip). It acts as the "DNA" of the device. It initializes the hardware, handles low-level inputs, and tells the device how to turn on.
* Example: The BIOS/UEFI on your computer motherboard, or the code inside a mouse that tells it how to track movement. [5, 6, 7, 8, 9] 
* Embedded Software: This is a broader category. It includes firmware, but it also includes the higher-level applications and operating systems that run on top of that hardware to perform complex tasks.
* Example: The Netflix app running on your smart TV, or the user interface on a smartwatch. [10, 11, 12, 13, 14] 

## Why Firmware Vulnerabilities Are Dangerous
Because firmware sits directly between the physical hardware and the operating system, it has the highest level of control. [15, 16, 17] 

* Hard to Detect: Standard antivirus software runs inside the operating system. It cannot scan the firmware layer underneath it.
* Persistent: If a hacker infects a device's firmware, formatting the hard drive or reinstalling the operating system will not remove the malware. The virus will reinstall itself every time the device boots up. [18, 19, 20, 21, 22] 

